--TEST--
Core\Arr::append derives the next free integer key from the entries it copies, where PHP's counter remembers a key that was removed and array_pop lowers it
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
An array's next free integer key is a property of its *history* in PHP and a property of its *entries* in Novis, and the difference is ADR 0063 R3's purity rather than an opinion about counters. Novis's own `$a[] = $v` mutates one array and does carry the counter across a removal, exactly as PHP's does; `Core\Arr::append` cannot, because it returns a fresh array and a fresh array has no history to inherit — its counter is one past the largest integer key actually present. PHP's `array_pop` goes further in the other direction and *lowers* the counter to the key it removed, so the same three entries can be followed by an append landing on three different keys depending on which statement produced them.
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Render {
    public static function of(array<int> $a): string {
        var $out = "";
        foreach ($a as string $k => int $v) {
            $out = $out . $k . "=" . $v . ";";
        }
        return $out;
    }
}

// The agreeing row, and the one that says the divergence is not about the
// counter's rule: `unset` leaves the counter where the largest key set put it,
// so a following `$a[] = $v` lands on 10 and not on 6. That is PHP's answer
// entry for entry.
array<int> $mutated = ["5" => 1, "x" => 2, "9" => 3];
unset($mutated["9"]);
$mutated[] = 9;
echo Render::of($mutated), "\n";

// The same three entries, the same removal, reached through the pure member
// instead. `Core\Arr::append` copies the subject into a new array, and that
// array's counter is derived from what was copied — 6, one past the largest
// integer key present. PHP's `$a[] = $v` on its own value would still say 10.
array<int> $pure = ["5" => 1, "x" => 2, "9" => 3];
unset($pure["9"]);
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::append($pure, 9)), "\n";

// `array_pop` does not merely keep the counter, it lowers it to the key it
// removed: PHP answers `5=1;x=2;9=9;` here, reusing the very key the pop freed.
// `Core\Arr::withoutLast` has no counter to lower, so composing it with
// `append` gives the same 6 the row above did.
array<int> $mixed = ["5" => 1, "x" => 2, "9" => 3];
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::append(Core\Arr::withoutLast($mixed), 9)), "\n";

// The same shape with the gap at the front rather than the back, so the two
// answers are far apart rather than adjacent: PHP's `array_pop` frees key 4
// and reuses it, while Novis numbers from the one entry that remains.
array<int> $gapped = ["0" => 1, "4" => 2];
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::append(Core\Arr::withoutLast($gapped), 9)), "\n";
--EXPECT--
5=1;x=2;10=9;
5=1;x=2;6=9;
5=1;x=2;6=9;
0=1;1=9;
